WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.900
Spider-Man has been featured in numerous movies
and hundreds of TV episodes, and many actors

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.940
have gotten a chance to portray the iconic
web-slinger.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:14.739
Here's a look at the major versions of Spider-Man
from TV and movies, as well as the actors

00:00:14.739 --> 00:00:18.920
who played them, ranked from worst to best.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.970
It isn't Nicholas Hammond's fault that barely
anybody remembers the short-lived "Amazing

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:29.269
Spider-Man" TV show from the late 1970s; he
just didn't have much to work with.

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:34.410
With no supervillains and only several familiar
faces from the comics, Hammond's take on the

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:39.190
character feels less like a superhero and
more like a regular guy in a red jumpsuit.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:44.570
"The Amazing Spider-Man" aired on CBS alongside
"The Incredible Hulk," but while the latter

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.789
show turned out to be a hit that defined the
character for years, "Amazing Spider-Man"

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:54.809
would only ever be a footnote in Spider-Man's
long history.

00:00:54.809 --> 00:00:59.219
"Spider-Man Unlimited" was an attempt to extend
the '90s animated series past its sell-by

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:04.630
date by recasting Spider-Man and sending him
to a darker alternate Earth.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:09.530
Rino Romano does very little to make the character
his own, instead making an effort to sound

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:13.060
exactly like his predecessor, Christopher
Daniel Barnes.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:19.300
His costume, inspired by the "Spider-Man 2099"
comics, certainly looks cool, but would probably

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.420
have fit better in an actual "Spider-Man 2099"
cartoon adaptation.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:31.100
Paul Soles as Spider-Man sounds the way people
in the 1960s expected a superhero to sound

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.710
— which means his voice is a little deeper
than the depictions of Peter Parker we see

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:36.710
today.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.990
Soles does his best getting into the role,
but it's clear that this first "Spider-Man"

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:46.800
cartoon, which ran from 1967 to 1970, isn't
very interested in characterization.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.240
This version of Spider-Man is perhaps best
remembered today as the star of many animated

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.240
GIFs.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:59.860
The 1981 "Spider-Man" animated series isn't
bad by any means: the animation is solid for

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.240
the time, and it brings in a lot of villains
from the comics who'd never gotten TV time

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.240
before.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.569
Ted Schwartz does an admirable job bringing
this iteration of Spider-Man to life, bringing

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:13.260
more individuality to the role than most had
before him.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:17.970
The strange thing about this show is that
it was immediately overshadowed by a different

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.880
Spider-Man cartoon.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:24.129
"Spider-Man and His Amazing Friends," which
premiered the same day, features more Marvel

00:02:24.129 --> 00:02:28.109
heroes and a stronger lead performance by
Dan Gilvezan.

00:02:28.109 --> 00:02:33.300
The two series also feature nearly identical
animation styles and character designs, which

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.480
can make for a disorienting viewing experience.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.879
Unfortunately for Schwartz, this is a series
that many have chosen to forget.

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:44.930
"Boy, some days it hardly pays to be a lovable
superhero."

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:51.329
Spider-Man's very first live-action appearance
was on "The Electric Company," a 1970s educational

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:53.290
series that ran on PBS.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:58.290
This Spider-Man doesn't talk and never appears
out of costume, but his simple adventures

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:04.150
and easily-described actions are the perfect
vehicle for teaching kids basic language and

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.150
math skills.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:10.090
The show also has a lot of fun bringing the
comic book aesthetic to life, with Danny Seagren

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:15.379
often framed by comic book panels and "speaking"
with the aid of word balloons.

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:21.719
It's a trick to get children to read, of course,
but it does so with plenty of fun and personality.

00:03:21.719 --> 00:03:25.019
Admittedly, this is kind of an odd one.

00:03:25.019 --> 00:03:29.730
In 1990, Spider-Man starred in a short PSA
about child abuse that was distributed to

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.370
schools and libraries.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:36.269
There's no Green Goblin or Mysterio here,
just a father who hits his daughter and a

00:03:36.269 --> 00:03:38.989
group of kids who turn to Spider-Man for help.

00:03:38.989 --> 00:03:44.310
Spider-Man tells them that violence is never
the answer and recommends a hotline to call.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:49.269
Spider-Man doesn't have a whole lot to do
here, since the spotlight is on the kids,

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:54.629
but he's well portrayed by veteran voice actor
Greg Snegoff, credited here as Gregory Snow.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.999
He's reminiscent of the '80s animated Spider-Man,
and, more importantly, has a warmth to him

00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:02.019
when interacting with troubled children.

00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:07.690
He comes off as an adult you can trust, which
is both perfect for this cartoon and exactly

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:10.510
what Spider-Man should be.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:15.959
Given time and a "Spider-Verse" sequel or
two, this Spider-Man of the future could potentially

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:18.500
move much higher on this list.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:24.220
After all, Spider-Man 2099, a.k.a. geneticist
Miguel O'Hara, has a cult following all his

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:25.220
own.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:30.740
On top of that, he's voiced by the incredibly
popular Oscar Isaac of "Star Wars" and "Ex

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:31.740
Machina" fame.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:37.060
The problem, of course, is that he's barely
in "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse" at

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:40.380
all, appearing only in the film's after-credits
sequence.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.700
It's fun to see him reenact a famous meme
from the 60's Spider-Man show, but it's an

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.920
inauspicious beginning for Miguel.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.430
We can only hope that he'll have more of a
presence in "Spider-Verse 2."

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:57.180
Two big things are distracting about 2003's
"Spider-Man: The New Animated Series," a more

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.070
mature take on the comics that aired on MTV,
of all places.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:06.130
The first is the animation, which combines
2D cartooning with 3D computer modeling in

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.720
an incredibly awkward way.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.421
The second is that every time Neil Patrick
Harris' version of Spider-Man talks, it's

00:05:12.421 --> 00:05:16.880
hard not to imagine Doogie Howser or Barney
Stinson under the red and blue suit.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.670
Harris wasn't a bad choice for Spider-Man
at the time, but it's difficult to accept

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:27.280
him as Peter Parker today when his voice has
become so recognizable as a musical TV star.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:34.340
Out of all the Spider-Man cartoons since the
60s, this one has probably aged the worst.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:38.890
Everybody loves Miles Morales, the modern
teenage Spider-Man created by Brian Michael

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:44.370
Bendis and Sara Pichelli in 2011 for Marvel's
now-defunct Ultimate Comics line.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:49.840
Even when the Ultimate line ended, Miles and
his supporting cast managed to survive, thanks

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:55.060
to his popularity and the unique flavor he
adds to the Spider-Man franchise.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.050
Unfortunately, bringing him into the 2017
"Spider-Man" animated series just didn't work

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:01.530
that well.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:05.430
Rather than originating from an alternate
dimension, this version of Miles, played by

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:10.160
Nadji Jeter, is nothing more than a school
friend of Peter Parker's who gains his own

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.430
powers and dons his own costume.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:17.490
When he first goes into action, he's portrayed
as a sloppy and overly enthusiastic hero who

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:23.250
initially does a very bad job — Peter has
to act as a coach and mentor, despite being

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:25.030
somewhere around Miles' age.

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:30.250
The whole storyline feels shoehorned in, and
robs Miles Morales of much of what makes him

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:33.340
such a compelling character.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:39.290
Gwen Stacy has been a part of Spider-Man's
story in the comics since 1965, but it's only

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:42.670
recently she's gained superpowers of her own.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.060
Known to the world as Spider-Woman and to
her friends as Spider-Gwen, this web-slinger

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.970
has been a fan favorite since she first donned
the white and pink suit in 2014.

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:57.500
The Gwen Stacy of the 2017 "Spider-Man'' series,
however, suffers from a similar problem as

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:02.470
Miles Morales: she's a schoolmate of Peter's,
rather than the main spider-person of her

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:03.880
own world.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:08.520
Unfortunately, this take on Gwen seems less
like her own superhero and more like just

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.810
another spidey-sidekick.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:15.570
Perhaps this show chose to avoid introducing
a multiverse because "Into The Spider-Verse"

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:20.910
was already in production, but both Spider-Gwen
and Miles Morales feel like cheap imitations

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.940
of their Spider-Verse incarnations as a result.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:30.280
Still, veteran voice actor Laura Bailey does
her best with the material she's been given.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:35.310
While Lego productions are known for drawing
heavily from their source material, 2015's

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:41.000
"Avengers Reassembled" actually came out before
Spider-Man's first MCU appearance in "Captain

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.120
America: Civil War."

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:48.080
With no Tom Holland performance to base Lego
Peter Parker off of, this plastic take on

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.700
Spidey is a bit generic compared to his counterparts
that aren't made out of bricks.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:57.720
Still, Ben Diskin gives it his all in the
role, as he continues to do in the role of

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.610
Flash Thompson in the 2017 "Spider-Man" series.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:06.270
Once the MCU established their own Spider-Man,
however, the Lego one had to evolve a bit

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.500
to keep up.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:13.980
Cole Howard is an experienced and talented
young voice actor, and his work in 2020's

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:18.730
"Lego Marvel Avengers: Climate Conundrum"
certainly fits the character of Peter Parker.

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:23.500
However, it's fairly clear that Howard got
the part because his Spider-Man voice sounds

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.810
very much like Tom Holland's.

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:30.770
Howard's Spider-Man comes off as a bit younger
and a lot sillier than Holland's live-action

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:34.499
performance, but the similarity is still unmistakable.

00:08:34.499 --> 00:08:39.539
For "Lego Marvel Avengers," which is basically
"the movies but sillier and aimed at younger

00:08:39.539 --> 00:08:44.050
audiences," Cole Howard is doing exactly what's
needed for the part.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:49.339
We're ranking the three spider-kids from this
Disney Junior series as a group rather than

00:08:49.339 --> 00:08:54.399
individually, because frankly these versions
just aren't quite distinctive enough to warrant

00:08:54.399 --> 00:08:55.839
their own entries.

00:08:55.839 --> 00:09:00.139
"Spidey and His Amazing Friends" is aimed
at preschool kids, which means all the sharp

00:09:00.139 --> 00:09:02.269
corners are pretty much sanded off.

00:09:02.269 --> 00:09:05.010
Of course, that doesn't mean it's a bad show.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:09.959
It's pleasant and likable enough, with each
episode featuring two short adventures in

00:09:09.959 --> 00:09:15.660
which the Spidey team face similarly aged-down
supervillains and learn lessons about sharing

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.009
and so forth.

00:09:17.009 --> 00:09:22.110
The villains include a really great female
Doc Ock who's basically a little girl version

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:24.269
of Kathryn Hahn from "Into the Spider-Verse."

00:09:24.269 --> 00:09:25.740
"Give it up, Doc Ock!

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.269
You can't handle the Spidey Team!"

00:09:28.269 --> 00:09:35.449
"But can you handle the might of Gigantapus
Squishy?!?"

00:09:35.449 --> 00:09:40.300
As far as characterization goes, however,
there's just not much going on in a show like

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:41.300
this.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:46.069
The Spidey Team is voiced by actual kids — and
talented ones at that — which does add to

00:09:46.069 --> 00:09:47.670
the show's unique energy.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:53.480
As kids' shows go, this one could certainly
be a lot worse, but it's not where adult fans

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:58.540
are going in search of great takes on Spider-Man
and his friends.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:02.931
The very first Spider-Man introduced in "Spider-Man:
Into The Spider-Verse" is the Peter Parker

00:10:02.931 --> 00:10:07.870
of Miles Morales' Earth, voiced by the absurdly
charismatic Chris Pine.

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:11.910
He's the big-time hero of New York, and basically
the perfect Spider-Man.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:17.860
He even has blond hair, unlike any other version
of Peter Parker, to mark him as a literal

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:18.860
golden boy.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:24.300
The problem, however, is that a flawless Spider-Man
doesn't feel like a Spider-Man at all: the

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:28.779
whole point of the character is that he's
a flawed hero and nothing ever seems to go

00:10:28.779 --> 00:10:29.829
right for him.

00:10:29.829 --> 00:10:33.860
This Spider-Man isn't in the movie to be a
character of his own, though: He's there to

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:38.970
provide an idealized image that conflicts
with the inexperienced Miles and the slovenly

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:40.310
Peter B. Parker.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:45.290
Plus, making him the perfect superhero makes
the tragedy of his death that much greater,

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:50.019
especially as he leaves behind a legacy that
Miles must try and live up to.

00:10:50.019 --> 00:10:55.100
Although the original "Spider-Man: Noir" comic
where this version of Peter Parker originated

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:59.269
is quite a serious affair, the version of
him that appears in "Into the Spider-Verse"

00:10:59.269 --> 00:11:02.070
is essentially a one-note joke character.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:07.519
Still, thanks to plenty of great lines and
a flawless voice performance by the peerless

00:11:07.519 --> 00:11:12.199
Nicolas Cage, he's an extremely funny joke
character.

00:11:12.199 --> 00:11:17.550
"Sometimes I let matches burn down to my fingertips
just to feel something, anything… aww.”

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:22.180
Not only does Spider-Man Noir sound like a
perfectly hard-boiled detective, he also looks

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:27.980
extremely striking, rendered entirely in black
and white halftone in a colorful world.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:32.899
If he comes back in "Spider-Verse 2," it might
be fun to get to know him a little better,

00:11:32.899 --> 00:11:38.370
but even if we don't, we're still incredibly
glad to have seen him in action.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:42.709
The 1978 Japanese "Spider-Man" live-action
TV series has developed a cult following in

00:11:42.709 --> 00:11:46.089
the U.S., even though it's never been officially
released here.

00:11:46.089 --> 00:11:50.399
It was produced by the Toei Company, who later
created "Mighty Morphin' Power Rangers," and

00:11:50.399 --> 00:11:53.930
they infused Spider-Man with a similar tokusatsu
vibe.

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:58.330
Shinji Tôdô plays Spider-Man's face and
voice, while Hirofumi Koga does much of the

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:00.949
performing inside the Spider-Protector suit.

00:12:00.949 --> 00:12:06.000
In this series, Spider-Man is a motorcycle
driver named Takuya Yamashiro, who gets his

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.309
spider-powers from a super-bracelet from the
planet Spider.

00:12:09.309 --> 00:12:13.619
Mostly, he does what American Spider-Man does
— climbing walls, spinning webs, and so

00:12:13.619 --> 00:12:19.309
forth — but being a true tokusatsu hero,
he also has a race car and a giant robot named

00:12:19.309 --> 00:12:20.309
Leopardon.

00:12:20.309 --> 00:12:25.600
The show's a lot of fun, and Tôdô and Koga
both do a great job, but it drifts pretty

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:28.519
far from Spider-Man as we understand him.

00:12:28.519 --> 00:12:33.019
These days, its primary appeal is definitely
the camp factor.

00:12:33.019 --> 00:12:39.860
A far more streamlined and spidery mech than
Leopardon, Peni Parker's SP//dr isn't nearly

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:42.449
as big, but it has a lot more personality.

00:12:42.449 --> 00:12:46.899
It's also co-piloted by an actual spider that
has a psychic link with young Peni, played

00:12:46.899 --> 00:12:48.309
by Kimiko Glenn.

00:12:48.309 --> 00:12:54.809
Together, they're the spider-heroes of a New
York in the year 3145 that exists somewhere

00:12:54.809 --> 00:12:56.720
out in the Spider-Verse.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:02.639
Peni and SP//dr are inspired by Japanese mecha
anime like "Neon Genesis Evangelion" and "Gundam."

00:13:02.639 --> 00:13:07.269
Although Peni doesn't get too much character
development, she's a great example of just

00:13:07.269 --> 00:13:12.199
how far a spider-hero can get from the standard
template we've seen time and time again.

00:13:12.199 --> 00:13:16.680
She also provides some emotional punch in
the final battle, as SP//dr sacrifices himself

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:17.860
to save Peni.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:22.670
Fortunately, she's able to save her spider
friend from inside the mech, so hopefully

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:26.629
they'll be able to rebuild upon returning
to their own dimension.

00:13:26.629 --> 00:13:32.490
Of all the secondary spider-people in "Spider-Man:
Into the Spider-Verse," Spider-Ham is by far

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:33.499
the silliest.

00:13:33.499 --> 00:13:36.149
That works for him, though — in fact, it's
kind of the whole point.

00:13:36.149 --> 00:13:41.310
Spider-Ham is essentially a Looney Tunes character,
down to his silhouette resembling Porky Pig.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:45.040
He's even mastered cartoon physics, making
him one of the most unpredictable fighters

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:47.410
the Spider-Verse has to offer.

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:51.429
"What are you, some kind of silly cartoon?"

00:13:51.429 --> 00:13:55.019
Spider-Ham's onscreen appearance is a departure
from the original comic book version of the

00:13:55.019 --> 00:13:59.550
character, who starred in 1985's "Peter Porker,
the Spectacular Spider-Ham."

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:04.769
There, he's a little more serious, working
alongside other animal Avengers like Captain

00:14:04.769 --> 00:14:07.350
AmeriCat and Iron Mouse.

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:12.429
In "Into the Spider-Verse," however, the silliness
of Spider-Ham makes for some of the movie's

00:14:12.429 --> 00:14:18.120
funniest moments, largely thanks to a fantastic
voice performance from comedian John Mulaney.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:23.671
By 2017, it was pretty established in the
minds of fans what a teenage Peter Parker

00:14:23.671 --> 00:14:27.220
should sound like, and Robbie Daymond nails
it.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:32.990
Aside from that, however, not much about this
version of the character feels fresh or new.

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:38.869
The 2017 "Spider-Man" is a solid, engaging
animated series, but it's all kind of been

00:14:38.869 --> 00:14:39.990
done already.

00:14:39.990 --> 00:14:44.759
It's fun to see Spider-Man team up with other
Marvel heroes, like Iron Man, Black Widow,

00:14:44.759 --> 00:14:50.000
and The Hulk, but storylines featuring multiple
web-slinging characters lack the spark that

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.589
made them memorable in "Into the Spider-Verse."

00:14:52.589 --> 00:14:57.820
Sometimes, it feels like the most daring part
of this "Spider-Man" incarnation is the way

00:14:57.820 --> 00:15:01.220
his belt doesn't quite fully connect on the
front of his costume.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:05.300
Still, if you're looking for a solid Spider-Man
voice performance, you could do a lot worse

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:07.220
than Robbie Daymond.

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:13.220
Back in the early days of 21st century superhero
movies, years before the MCU, Tobey Maguire

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:14.970
got the job done.

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:19.970
He got in great physical shape for Sam Raimi's
"Spider-Man" and its sequels, but kept the

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:25.319
charmingly goofy face that made Peter Parker
a relatable figure for audiences of all ages.

00:15:25.319 --> 00:15:30.471
While he never quite seems like a kid the
way an early career Spider-Man should, he's

00:15:30.471 --> 00:15:36.150
young-looking enough to sell a teenage performance
— even though he was in his late 20s when

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:37.379
production began.

00:15:37.379 --> 00:15:41.749
In the emotional scenes, Maguire's always
a little over the top, but he makes up for

00:15:41.749 --> 00:15:44.720
it with fun quips and killer dance moves.

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:48.819
He's definitely a solid Spider-Man, but nothing
to write home about.

00:15:48.819 --> 00:15:52.740
"Ultimate Spider-Man" was controversial from
day one.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:58.199
For one thing, it replaced the popular "Spectacular
Spider-Man'' series, which ended due to character

00:15:58.199 --> 00:16:01.619
rights issues after Disney purchased Marvel
in 2009.

00:16:01.619 --> 00:16:06.899
Some fans grew even more irritated when Drake
Bell of "Drake and Josh" fame was hired to

00:16:06.899 --> 00:16:11.069
voice the title character, expecting that
this version of the character would be geared

00:16:11.069 --> 00:16:13.420
for a younger, less mature audience.

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:18.470
However, if you ignore any bad publicity,
Bell is actually a pretty great animated Spider-Man.

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:23.410
He has a high capacity for silliness and fun,
as Spider-Man always should, but he also comes

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:27.679
through in the darker moments that require
a more emotional Peter Parker.

00:16:27.679 --> 00:16:31.879
Although this iteration of Spider-Man may
not have gotten the same rave reviews that

00:16:31.879 --> 00:16:37.689
"Spectacular Spider-Man's" Peter Parker did,
Bell certainly does the job.

00:16:37.689 --> 00:16:41.820
The biggest problem with Andrew Garfield in
"The Amazing Spider-Man" is that he always

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:44.459
looked too old for the part.

00:16:44.459 --> 00:16:48.399
Garfield may have made a good adult Peter
Parker if the script wrote him as such, but

00:16:48.399 --> 00:16:52.519
a nearly thirty-year old man in the role of
a bullied high schooler is a hard sell to

00:16:52.519 --> 00:16:55.579
the point that it almost never stops being
distracting.

00:16:55.579 --> 00:16:59.899
Even still, Garfield's take on the wall crawler
has plenty going for him: he looks good in

00:16:59.899 --> 00:17:04.400
the suit, his comedic timing is on point,
and his chemistry with Emma Stone's Gwen Stacy

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:09.159
makes for one of the best romantic storylines
that Spider-Man media has to offer.

00:17:09.159 --> 00:17:13.679
Garfield is a fine transitional Spider-Man,
but not exactly the type that would have worked

00:17:13.679 --> 00:17:16.899
best in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:17:16.899 --> 00:17:22.059
For a lot of millennials, Christopher Daniel
Barnes was the definitive voice of Spider-Man

00:17:22.059 --> 00:17:27.819
during their childhood, having played the
role on Fox Kids from 1994 to 1998.

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:32.740
While this "Spider-Man" series has aged a
little awkwardly, it was the first Spider-Man

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:38.000
TV show to draw on the comics' storylines
in a nuanced way, introducing characters like

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.590
Venom and even the Punisher to Saturday morning
viewers.

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:47.470
This series saves the origin story for the
theme song and begins with Peter Parker already

00:17:47.470 --> 00:17:50.519
in college and freelancing for the Daily Bugle.

00:17:50.519 --> 00:17:55.640
Within that context, Barnes is perfect in
the part, balancing the jokes and the emotional

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:59.049
baggage that are both so central to Spider-Man.

00:17:59.049 --> 00:18:05.330
With its solid characterization and season-long
story arcs, this show is one of the best takes

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:08.470
on grown-up Peter Parker that you can find.

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:14.190
For those a little older, Dan Gilvezan is
the Saturday morning Spider-Man of choice.

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:19.390
He voices the wall crawling hero on "Spider-Man
and His Amazing Friends," a show that brought

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:23.840
the Marvel universe to life from 1981 until
1983.

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:30.010
Gilvezan was a mainstay of 1980s animation,
also known for his roles as Bumblebee in "Transformers"

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:32.090
and Cooler in "Pound Puppies."

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:36.289
His Spider-Man has the lightness that earlier
versions of the character had been missing,

00:18:36.289 --> 00:18:41.890
while still seeming more grounded than his
goofier friend Iceman, played by Frank Welker.

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:47.370
This cartoon may seem pretty silly by today's
standards, but this was the first time Spider-Man

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:53.669
really felt like the Spider-Man we all know
today, and that's worth something.

00:18:53.669 --> 00:18:58.320
Despite failing to gain momentum in the 2017
cartoon, Spider-Gwen thankfully got her chance

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:02.780
to shine as one of the numerous spider-people
featured in "Into the Spider-Verse."

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:07.960
As the one and only spider-hero of her world,
this incarnation of Gwen Stacy was motivated

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:12.470
to protect New York City by the death of her
best friend Peter Parker before getting sucked

00:19:12.470 --> 00:19:15.110
into an extra-dimensional wormhole.

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:20.250
Despite rocky first impressions, Gwen becomes
a close friend to Miles, and despite a hint

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:25.940
of romantic tension, there's nothing too forced
or mushy to distract from daring heroics.

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:30.769
As far as spider-people go, Spider-Gwen is
one of the strongest: while Miles is new to

00:19:30.769 --> 00:19:36.210
the game and Peter B.'s glory days are behind
him, Gwen is in the prime of her superhero

00:19:36.210 --> 00:19:40.770
career: she's graceful, strong, and always
helpful in a pinch.

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:46.980
Hailee Steinfeld gives a standout voice performance
as Gwen, with the perfect mix of youthful

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:52.960
enthusiasm and effortless ability: she's not
exactly Spider-Man, but as far as spider-people

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:55.470
go, she's one of the best.

00:19:55.470 --> 00:20:00.440
Peter Parker has always been a bit of a sad
sack, but Peter B. Parker from "Into the Spider-Verse"

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:02.309
takes it to a whole new level.

00:20:02.309 --> 00:20:06.710
As the hero of New York for over two decades,
the years have taken their toll on Peter — as

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:11.770
he approaches 40, he finds himself broke,
separated from his wife Mary Jane, and gaining

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.850
a noticeable paunch.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:19.340
He's the natural endpoint of Peter Parker's
bad luck, and the exact opposite of the Spider-Man

00:20:19.340 --> 00:20:21.230
that Miles Morales grew up seeing.

00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:26.090
However, once he realizes it's up to him to
mentor young Miles in the art of web-slinging,

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:29.450
he rediscovers the hero he's always been deep
down.

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:33.990
This is a take on Peter Parker that's never
quite been seen before, and wouldn't work

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:39.960
in most contexts — but in "Spider-Man: Into
the Spider-Verse," he's perfect, hilarious,

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:41.279
and ultimately touching.

00:20:41.279 --> 00:20:46.510
Jake Johnson, star of TV's "New Girl," is
the perfect choice to voice this version of

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:50.490
the character, and gives a surprisingly nuanced
performance.

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:57.639
He may not be the best or most powerful Spider-Man,
but he might just be the most endearing one.

00:20:57.639 --> 00:21:04.050
"Spectacular Spider-Man" is considered by
many to be the perfect 21st century Spider-Man

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:08.919
cartoon — and one that ended far too soon,
for frustrating reasons.

00:21:08.919 --> 00:21:14.389
It ran for two seasons from 2008 to 2009,
and was being considered for a third when

00:21:14.389 --> 00:21:20.040
Disney bought Marvel and reacquired the television
rights to Spider-Man, leaving this Sony-produced

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:23.279
series in legal limbo, unable to continue.

00:21:23.279 --> 00:21:27.519
"Spectacular Spider-Man" takes Peter Parker
back to high school, with a unique new design

00:21:27.519 --> 00:21:29.920
aesthetic that fits perfectly.

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:34.830
Voice actor Josh Keaton plays this youthful
Spider-Man with the earnestness of a kid trying

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:37.830
to do his best, and he couldn't have done
a better job.

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:41.539
It's just a shame we couldn't have seen more
of him.

00:21:41.539 --> 00:21:46.309
Despite being the third live-action Spidey
inside of a decade, Tom Holland's take on

00:21:46.309 --> 00:21:51.830
Peter Parker was a fan favorite from the moment
the world first saw him steal the vibranium

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:55.130
shield in the trailer for "Captain America:
Civil War."

00:21:55.130 --> 00:22:00.320
Unlike Maguire and Garfield, however, Holland
has the authentic look and feel of an anxious

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:03.779
teenager who's gotten himself way in over
his head.

00:22:03.779 --> 00:22:09.409
The genuine youthfulness and naiveté of Holland's
portrayal of Peter Parker contrasts perfectly

00:22:09.409 --> 00:22:14.270
with the adult heroes of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, especially Iron Man.

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:19.090
Tony Stark's mentorship of Peter adds serious
emotional depth to both characters in a way

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:21.559
no other version of Spider-Man has done.

00:22:21.559 --> 00:22:26.669
While Spider-Man is a character defined by
tragedy, no other actors have captured the

00:22:26.669 --> 00:22:32.159
raw emotion of Peter Parker quite as effectively
as Holland has — as we've witnessed in one

00:22:32.159 --> 00:22:36.830
of the most emotionally devastating moments
the MCU has to offer.

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:43.120
"I don't wanna go, I don't wanna go, sir,
please, please I don't wanna go, I don't wanna

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:44.120
go…"

00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:48.600
As far as his solo efforts go, Holland's Peter
Parker is an awkward, yet charismatic young

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:53.220
hero who's still finding his place in the
world, trying to balance the life of an honor

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.880
student with the life of a superhero.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:59.940
Even though he's constantly having to clean
up his own mistakes wherever he goes, he comes

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:05.450
across as inexperienced without ever seeming
incompetent, and by the end of "Far From Home,"

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:08.289
he's become a hero that Tony Stark would be
proud of.

00:23:08.289 --> 00:23:13.140
All of this is topped off with a technological
marvel of a costume, which gives Spider-Man

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:17.769
the opportunity to be more expressive than
any other live-action version of him had been

00:23:17.769 --> 00:23:18.769
before.

00:23:18.769 --> 00:23:23.730
Details like this — combined with Tom Holland's
stellar performance — make for the best

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:27.669
Peter Parker outside of the comics themselves.

00:23:27.669 --> 00:23:32.960
With apologies to all versions of Peter Parker,
Miles Morales is the Spider-Man of the future.

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:37.820
He's energetic, he's artistic, and as the
son of a Black father and a Puerto Rican mother,

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:42.130
he represents the changing face of America
in the best possible light.

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:46.540
As someone who grew up in Spider-Man's friendly
neighborhood, Miles finds himself with some

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:50.659
pretty big shoes to fill after the death of
his world's Peter Parker.

00:23:50.659 --> 00:23:55.730
However, with some help from his family and
a half-dozen other spider-people, Miles is

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:59.730
able to figure out his abilities and become
the hero his city needs.

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:04.721
It's more than just his background that makes
Miles' Spider-Man unique, however — his

00:24:04.721 --> 00:24:11.130
invisibility and venom strike abilities eventually
make him a fearsome foe for baddies like Kingpin.

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:16.299
Despite all the spider-people who appear throughout
"Into the Spider-Verse," it's Miles' story

00:24:16.299 --> 00:24:17.509
first and foremost.

00:24:17.509 --> 00:24:23.419
And just as it's the best Spider-Man movie
ever made, it shows how Miles has the potential

00:24:23.419 --> 00:24:25.070
to be the best Spider-Man.

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:29.840
It certainly helps that he's voiced by Shameik
Moore from "The Get Down," who embodies the

00:24:29.840 --> 00:24:31.580
character flawlessly.

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:35.730
It's only a matter of time until we see Miles
in live-action, whether he's replacing Peter

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:37.470
Parker or working alongside him.

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:41.730
Miles Morales is the future of Spider-Man,
and the future is bright.

